{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/round69-brief.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Council round 69 (what goes to Alex in the morning): independent read, read-only\n     2\t\n     3\tYou are an independent reviewer. Do not edit any file. The repository is /Users/lchingen/hivemind. Cite file:line.\n     4\t\n     5\t## Context\n     6\t- **D751's exit rule fired.** `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND7-RESULT.md` (with `BLIND6-RESULT.md` §2,\n     7\t  `BLIND5-RESULT.md`), `SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D751], `codex-r67.md`, `codex-r68.md`. A settled failure on E's Claude writer ended\n     8\t  the wording loop, and \"the publication choice for E-type cases returns to Alex\".\n     9\t- **Alex is asleep.** D439 governs: $0, reversible. Only Alex can authorize paid work, and he conditioned the paid re-send on a\n    10\t  clean $0 pass (D749 in `docs/DECISIONS-log.md`).\n    11\t- **The paid gate's lines** are in `PAID-GATE.md` §4. Its singleton S case asks about CY2027 (`paid_gate.py` SINGLETONS).\n    12\t- **Claude's proposal for the morning** (to attack). Two questions, each with options and a recommendation:\n    13\t  1. **The E-type publication default.** \"When a company's reaffirmed target sits exactly on a band line and nothing in the\n    14\t     sources sizes a shortfall or an overshoot, what should the system show?\"\n    15\t     - (a) \"can't tell yet\", naming the missing fact (recommended). Both reader families accepted it in all three runs where a\n    16\t       writer gave it.\n    17\t     - (b) the band of the company's target, by default.\n    18\t     - Claude's reason for (a): D725 \"don't hallucinate with the numbers\", D745 (\"a justified unresolved answer counts\"), and the\n    19\t       existing line-9 unresolved sentence already states it. A rung-2 guidance-in-full answer split (D749).\n    20\t  2. **The next step.**\n    21\t     - Authorize the paid re-send now (36 calls, ≤ $0.50, the D751 request), waiving D749's clean-$0 condition (recommended).\n    22\t     - Or stop the line, and production keeps \"Forecast unavailable\" for number questions (D726).\n    23\t     - Claude's reasons:\n    24\t       - the remaining failures are one proxy writer family's behaviour on boundary cases;\n    25\t       - P (the pair carrying D725's hypothesis) and the controls are solid;\n    26\t       - only the paid run measures luna;\n    27\t       - a further $0 wording round is what the exit rule forbids.\n    28\t\n    29\t## Questions\n    30\t1. Is the morning proposal faithful to the record and to the exit rule? Is anything overstated?\n    31\t2. Is question 1 the right product question, balanced and general? Is (a) the right recommendation? Does (a) need any change to the\n    32\t   request, or does line 9 already state it?\n    33\t3. Is recommending the paid re-send right, given Alex's own clean-pass condition and what the paid run can still decide (compute\n    34\t   the attainable verdict from `PAID-GATE.md` §4)? Or should the recommendation be different?\n    35\t4. Return the final wording of the two questions and their options, in plain language, with your recommended option for each.\n    36\t\n    37\tReturn a numbered report with citations.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r69.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t**Recommend “can’t tell yet” when the evidence leaves both sides open, and keep the paid test on hold.** On the same sales question, one writer said it needed orders or a delivery schedule to choose a range. Another chose 85% growth and forecast $37B. Both readers accepted the first answer and rejected the second because the sources gave no basis for choosing 85%. [Codex answer:83](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind7-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:83) · [BLIND7-RESULT.md:19](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND7-RESULT.md:19)\n     2\t\n     3\t1. **The wording rounds ended. The paid test did not earn approval.** D751 specified two outcomes. A clean pass would earn a request for paid authorization. A settled failure would end the wording rounds and return the publication choice to Alex. The final test produced that settled failure. Alex can now choose to waive his earlier condition, but describing a waiver as “the D751 request” gives it authority the decision did not grant. [SMALL-DESIGN.md:580](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:580) · [BLIND7-RESULT.md:27](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND7-RESULT.md:27) · [DECISIONS-log.md:11645](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11645)\n     4\t\n     5\t   Claude’s account is accurate about the three “can’t tell” answers. Both reader families accepted all three, but all came from the Codex writer on the **same case and passages**. The $42B answer taking guidance in full split the readers. The spending forecast also drew disagreements, including two disputed defects in the preceding round. Both writers consistently produced supported forecasts for the Data Center question, and readers caught all six planted errors across the last three rounds. Those checks establish neither that calculating the two revenue forecasts together helps nor how often Luna would make either mistake. [BLIND7-RESULT.md:30](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND7-RESULT.md:30) · [BLIND5-RESULT.md:42](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND5-RESULT.md:42) · [BLIND6-RESULT.md:48](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND6-RESULT.md:48)\n     6\t\n     7\t2. **Ask whether the evidence favours a range, rather than whether a source gives the exact size of a miss.** Alex already permits a forecast when a relevant comparison explains why one side is more likely, even if the other remains possible. Guidance standing alone also has its own rule. The open choice concerns a target **at or near a cutoff**, where other evidence bears on the forecast but still leaves the choice open. That wording also fits the actual example more closely. Management expected *more than double* a projected $20B, so $40B was the implied lower edge of its outlook. [DECISIONS-log.md:11575](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11575) · [judging-v13s.txt:4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:4) · [E source passages:10](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind7-2026-11-23/E/user.txt:10)\n     8\t\n     9\t   I recommend **(a)**. Line 9 already tells the writer to say it cannot choose and name the missing fact when the evidence only shows that sales *might* fall short or exceed the target. Alex explicitly endorsed that behaviour. Choosing (a) needs no request edit. The failed test shows that a writer can still disregard the instruction. Choosing (b) would establish a new publication policy. [judging-v13s.txt:9](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:9) · [DECISIONS-log.md:11645](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11645) · [DECISIONS-log.md:11566](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11566)\n    10\t\n    11\t3. **Keep the paid test on hold, even though it can still give a meaningful result.** A complete run can pass in exactly two ways: **8 of 9** usable pairs calculated together against **at most 5** calculated separately, or **9 of 9** against **at most 6** separately. Each of the three snapshots needs at least two usable pairs calculated together. If separate calculation gets seven, even a perfect nine cannot pass. One published contradiction, one internal forecast presented as the market’s view, or one unsupported adjustment or scope change that decides the range stops a complete run on any joint or standalone answer. A standalone answer judged unusable does not by itself trigger that stop. Missing required readings or an unsent unit leave the result incomplete. [PAID-GATE.md:259](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:259) · [PAID-GATE.md:270](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:270) · [paid_gate.py:772](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:772)\n    12\t\n    13\t   The free spending question asked about **2026**. The paid one asks about **2027**. The free disagreement cannot settle that result in advance. A paid run could tell Alex whether this version of Luna benefits from calculating related forecasts together. Even a pass would earn further design work only. That is a reason to leave a one-time exception available to Alex, but the evidence gives me no reason to recommend dropping the condition he expressly chose. [SMALL-DESIGN.md:169](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:169) · [paid_gate.py:44](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:44) · [PAID-GATE.md:278](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:278) · [DECISIONS-log.md:11651](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11651)\n    14\t\n    15\t4. **The two questions for Alex**\n    16\t\n    17\t   **Question 1. A company repeats a target near the cutoff between two forecast ranges. After weighing the target and the other evidence, we still can’t say which range is more likely. What should readers see?**\n    18\t\n    19\t   - **A — recommended.** Show the company’s target as context. Say “We can’t tell yet” and name the fact that would help us choose, such as how much customers have ordered for delivery this year.\n    20\t   - **B.** Use the company’s target range as our default forecast. Tell readers that the forecast relies on management meeting its target. [judging-v13s.txt:9](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:9) · [DECISIONS-log.md:11566](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11566)\n    21\t\n    22\t   **Question 2. The last free test produced a sales forecast that both readers rejected. Do you want to keep the paid comparison on hold or make a one-time exception?**\n    23\t\n    24\t   - **A — recommended.** Stop here and spend $0. Keep the agreed first-release plan: readers see the number question, its sources and “Forecast unavailable.”\n    25\t   - **B.** Waive the clean-pass condition once. Authorize 36 scored requests to Luna, with any diagnostic retries inside the same **$0.50 total cap**, to see whether calculating related forecasts together gives more usable answers. A pass would justify designing the production change. [DECISIONS-log.md:11004](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11004) · [PAID-GATE.md:21](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:21) · [PAID-GATE.md:35](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:35) · [PAID-GATE.md:278](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:278)","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"wc -l docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md && sed -n '1,95p' docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md\"","aggregated_output":"     201 docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md\n# Core PRD for the next phase: filing must recognise indirect relations the way an analyst does\n\n> Status: in progress (opened 2026-11-04). Alex: \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\".\n> This page is the single source for this phase. In a conflict: Alex's words → `docs/DECISIONS.md` → this page → the other design documents. This page's\n> conclusions, evidence, constraints, measuring method and process are not rewritten to follow test results;\n> test results go into `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md`, and changing this page first needs Alex's word.\n> Amended 2026-11-04 (Alex replied \"go\" to the proposed amendment; D360): §5.2 drops \"also file under the other party's question\" and puts two\n> sentences in its place, one on choosing and one on splitting; §6's decision rule is rewritten. How it came about:\n> `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/prd-amendment-proposal.md` and `log.md` rows 11–16.\n> 2026-11-05 (D368): translated into English on Alex's word (\"不，用英文吧\"); §4 corrected to name the frozen standard's version 5 and §5 given a status line, on his delegation (\"你要来帮我看到底做不做\").\n\n## 0. Checklist before every start (Alex: \"每次开始前都需要revisit\")\n\nBefore starting any step, finish these in order; if one cannot be done, do not start:\n\n1. Read §§1–8 of this page in full; read the last three rows of `log.md`, and confirm where the previous step stopped and why.\n2. Check against §3, \"not in scope\": what this step does is not in that table. If it is in the table, stop and take it to Alex.\n3. Check against §4, \"constraints\": this step changes nothing frozen; a change to the frozen extraction prompt is the one place §5 names, and Alex's\n   word for it is already given.\n4. If this step costs money: its predictions and stop conditions are written down and frozen as §7 says (with their sha256 recorded beside them); the\n   requests are rebuilt byte for byte as §6 says; the control is re-sent the same day.\n5. What goes to Alex: one conclusion, one recommendation, one test, after §7's rounds of review; no menu of options; in plain words (the opening of\n   AGENTS, \"Explain and answer as to a sixteen-year-old\").\n\n## 1. Goal (end to end)\n\nEach question's judgement must receive the facts that can change its answer; facts that no question receives, those that bear on no question, must\nnot be piled into some one question. What is measured is the final judgement (the frozen judgement ruler, reader B, at the window end 2026-07-14), not\nany one step's own score.\n\n## 2. The core blocker (proven)\n\n**The system uses a fact for a question only when the fact \"states exactly this question's own figure\". An analyst also reasons onward from a fact: the other party's figure in a deal, price and volume for a product's revenue, a product's variants for its launch, and so on. The filing step cannot do this; the judging step reasons onward once it has the facts.**\n\nThis one blocker has two faces (described earlier as \"two problems\"):\n\n- **The parking lot.** Filing does not recognise facts that bear on a question indirectly, so no question takes them; yet to be kept, a fact must\n  first be filed (D209, D264). So D273 added the fallback sentence, which parks them in NVIDIA's total-revenue question. Of the reports under the\n  total-revenue question, in the part the gold read, 32–39% are ones the standard does not keep, 31–44% are kept but used by none of the 22 questions,\n  and only 7–10% are what the gold actually uses to judge total revenue (three draws); 24–44% of the total-revenue question's judgement citations are\n  news about other companies or the whole market (Intel graphics-card retail prices, a gas-fired power plant in Taiwan).\n- **Narrow questions starve.** Questions that only indirect facts can answer do not receive them: the custom-chip question received none of Broadcom's\n  figures in any of the three draws, and judged \"below $40 billion\" in all three; the operating-margin and NVIDIA capital-spending questions received\n  nothing at all and were never judged.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n| Evidence | Content | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Filing recognises only \"its own figure\" | The model says \"Broadcom's AI chip revenue is not cloud providers' spending on custom chips\" and \"Google Cloud's backlog is not capital spending\"; on a neutral refiling (no question id named, the rule not shown), 23 of 42 facts that matter are still not filed | `probe-filing-account-d1.md`, `refile-no_fallback.json` |\n| The judgement reasons onward once it has the facts | The custom-chip question's request of 7-14, sent 3 times in each of the three draws: as it was, 9/9 \"below $40 billion\"; with the Broadcom, Trainium and other facts written in that draw's ledger added, 9/9 left the lowest rung (6 times $40–60 billion, 3 times $60–80 billion), reasoning as the two readers did (\"guidance $16 billion, about $64 billion annualised, less networking chips\") | `predictions-one-test.md`, `one-test-result.json`, `log.md` row 8 |\n| Where the parking lot came from | D273: with the fallback sentence added, the core items on the extraction ruler went 64 → 68; the extraction ruler does not look at which question a fact is filed under (AGENTS §7.1, \"What it does not measure\") | `docs/DECISIONS.md` D273 |\n| What the parking lot holds | The figures above; an independent reader on 12 random articles: of 25 misfilings as it was, 21 were parked in the revenue question; 8 and 5 with the fallback deleted | `sink_by_standard.py`, the independent reader's report (`log.md` row 8) |\n\nAll files above are in `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/` unless noted otherwise.\n\n## 3. Ruled out or secondary (outside this phase's scope, each recorded separately)\n\n| Matter | Conclusion | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| The argument card | Not built; its code is deleted (Alex: \"好，删掉卡片代码\") | `docs/research/2026-11-04-argument-record/log.md` rows 4–11 |\n| The arrows of the question graph | Really wired backwards: a seed question's arrow means \"supports\" (D208), while the judging code reads it as \"whose conclusion to read\" (PRD 06). As a result themes are judged only after the total-revenue question, so the total-revenue question reads the themes' conclusions of the previous day, and in draw 3 a chain of questions went wrong in turn. But it does not explain the misfiling (filing comes before judging), and the gold itself judges data-center revenue as \"total revenue × 92%\". A topic of its own; settled by D368 (item 4: the arrow means \"supports\"), its fix to be built and measured | `core-problem.md`, `review-core-codex.md` |\n| Code removes the total-revenue question's own figures | True (\"keep only the narrowest question\" takes a seed arrow for \"a narrower sub-question\"), but each draw it costs the total-revenue question only 0–2 of the facts it needs | `log.md` row 8 |\n| A filing re-check added at the end | Recovers some, and misfiles about as many (independent reader: about one misfiled for each one filed right); not the solution | The independent reader's report |\n| One general sentence, \"the other party's figure in a deal counts too\" | The custom-chip question gains, but it pulls Morgan Stanley's price for a Rubin rack from the Rubin revenue question to the capital-spending question, and leaves some facts with no home | `answers.json` (the two arms definition and definition_no_fallback), `measure-corrected.txt` |\n| Tightening D259's \"leeway\" | Alex's own decision; this phase does not overturn it | D259 |\n| Opening questions (the Ohio guarantee was not opened); the two readers' disagreement on the China-share question | Another class of problem, not in this phase | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/diagnosis.md` §0 |\n| Model drift | With the same requests, today about half as many facts go into the total-revenue question as on 11-03; the cause is not proven. Compare only with a control re-sent the same day, never with the archive | `log.md` row 6 |\n\n## 4. Constraints (what must not move, and why)\n\n- **The frozen ruler** (AGENTS §7.1, D261, D262): the extraction standard, version 5 (the signed version 3 with D248's seven boundaries and D250's four\n  evidence boundaries and counting unit), golden-v5, the seed questions and the judgement gold; not a word changes; a disagreement is a gap in the\n  system (D263).\n- **The frozen extraction prompt** (D274): v74i (`poc-v108`) does not change by a word; what this phase changes is the one place that holds the filing\n  rule (the description of the `links` field), made into a new version with a new configuration and compared with v74i. Alex has given his word for this\n  work (2026-11-04: \"好你去解决问题\", \"开始做\"); making it the default needs his word again.\n- **Frozen opening and judging** (D354): this phase does not touch them.\n- **The standard's text** (in translation): §6, \"file under the narrowest open question; when no question takes it, record no home and drop it, and read\n  each one at evaluation\"; §5.1, \"makes the answer to some question more or less likely\", with four paths, \"demand, supply, competition, pricing\"; §5.3,\n  \"another company's matter is not kept when one cannot say which question about the covered company it moves through a path such as demand, supply,\n  competition or pricing\".\n- **Decisions**: D207 (one home per fact, the narrowest question), D209 (no home means dropped, each one read), D259 (leeway: a figure question takes\n  \"the demand, supply, competition and pricing that make it more or less likely\"; the total-revenue question taking more is an accepted cost, \"bounded by\n  the narrowest-question rule and the driver questions\"), D265 (code keeps only the narrowest), D273 (the fallback sentence), D355 (the two failed tests\n  of the previous filing topic).\n- **Prompt principles** (AGENTS §6): carry only the standard and add no new rule; a general hedge-fund analyst: every rule and example reads the same\n  for a bank, a drug maker, a retailer or a chip company, names neither NVIDIA nor its products or figures, and is not taken from a test article; a\n  classification is written as a closed set with two or three examples per class, replacing the abstract wording; quality is not fixed by lengthening\n  the prompt: remove as much as is added, or write down the reason.\n- **Code decides only \"is it the same thing\", never \"does it matter\"** (AGENTS §2).\n\n## 5. What to do\n\nStatus: the links-rule version that carried items 1–3 (v74l) failed its one test (§8 step 5); D361 keeps the fallback sentence, so item 3 is not carried out. The plan below is kept as written.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
