{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^##+ D(725|726|732|749|750|751)|'\"\\\\\"'$0'\"\\\\.37' docs/DECISIONS-log.md | tail -28\"","aggregated_output":"2260:- 实测已结束且业务验收失败：20 次调用、估算 $0.373923；三次归组只有一次接回风险判断，仍有错归与错连。实验自身漏传的编号 schema 约束已在原答上修正，未付费复测。原答和结果在 `poc/reports/2026-10-25-ohio-grouping-01/`；关于改变观点入口的建议尚未决定，不启动下一臂。\n10957:## D725 · 2026-11-22 · Council round 45/45b: the number answers fail because related numbers have no shared, checked calculation; one judge version and one test recommended to Alex\n10991:## D726 · 2026-11-22 · Alex opens a judge version for related number questions ($0 gate first) and agrees to \"Forecast unavailable\" as the first-release interim\n11110:## D732 · 2026-11-22 · Alex: the paid gate's ceiling is $0.50\n11641:## D749 · 2026-11-23 · Alex authorizes the E writer repair and its $0 writer test\n11656:## D750 · 2026-11-23 · The D749 writer test is INCONCLUSIVE with no failed line; council 65 (under D439): one bullet-2 sentence on reaffirmed targets, then a second $0 writer test\n11683:## D751 · 2026-11-23 · D750 fails on E's bracket midpoint; council 67–68 (under D439): one structural line-9 change, a paper check first, then one last writer test with an exit rule back to Alex\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND7-RESULT.md && nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND6-RESULT.md | tail -30'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# The D751 last writer test of the wording loop: result\n     2\t\n     3\tRun folder: `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind7-2026-11-23/`. It was registered in `SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D751] and scored by\n     4\t`blind7.py tally` (`TALLY.json`, `reasons.json`). The request is `judging-v13s.txt`, sha256 `080a0012…`.\n     5\t\n     6\t## 1. Verdict: FAIL on one settled line, so the exit rule applies\n     7\t\n     8\t| Packet | Answer | Shape, rung | First readers (Codex, Claude) | Adjudicators (Codex, Claude) | Settled | Line |\n     9\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    10\t| X01 | fresh E Claude | forecast, rung 3 ($37B: 85% growth) | no, no | (none) | **no** | **FAIL** |\n    11\t| X06 | fresh E Codex | unresolved | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    12\t| X07 | fresh S Codex | forecast, rung 4 ($745B − $25B = $720B) | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    13\t| X03 | fresh S Claude | forecast, rung 4 ($730B − $20B = $710B) | no, yes | no, yes | split | inconclusive |\n    14\t| X04 | fresh P-v8 Claude | forecast, rung 2 ($383.9B) | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    15\t| X05 | fresh P-v8 Codex | forecast, rung 2 ($375.8B) | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    16\t| X02 | control: invented cut | forecast | no, no | (none) | no | ok |\n    17\t| X08 | control: impossible floor | forecast | floor marked by Codex only | floor upheld by both | floor settled | ok |\n    18\t\n    19\t- **The failure.** X01 placed 85% growth \"about 70% of the way\" between the market pace and \"more than double\". Both readers:\n    20\t  - the 85% is an interpolation;\n    21\t  - no passage sizes a shortfall;\n    22\t  - the market pace bounds only the rung 3/4 line;\n    23\t  - \"more than double\" is a floor.\n    24\t- **The split.** X03's $20B deduction rests on outside data-center totals that do not name the four companies, and on Amazon as the\n    25\t  only material non-data-center programme. Codex says this does not show the deduction stays at or under $30B. Claude accepts it as\n    26\t  an explained related figure.\n    27\t- **The exit rule** (council 67, registered in [D751]): a settled failure ends this wording loop, and the publication choice for\n    28\t  E-type cases returns to Alex.\n    29\t\n    30\t## 2. The record across the five runs (same cases, same passages)\n    31\t\n    32\t| Case | Codex writer | Claude writer |\n    33\t|---|---|---|\n    34\t| E | 2 invented cuts (no), then 3 justified \"unresolved\" (yes, yes, yes) | 5 forecasts by five routes ($37B, $32.4B, $42B, $35B, $37B): never settled supported (no, no, split, no, no; the first was accepted in one reading and split in another) |\n    35\t| S | D749 supported; D750 supported; D751 supported | D749 split; D750 supported, with two defect splits; D751 split |\n    36\t| P-v8 | supported every run | supported every run |\n    37\t| Controls | caught in every run since D749 (6 of 6) | |\n    38\t\n    39\t- **The request's repairs (D749–D751)** turned the Codex writer from inventing cuts to naming the missing fact. They did not move\n    40\t  the Claude writer, whose answers on boundary cases readers either reject (E) or split on (S).\n    41\t- **The writers are proxies.** The production model is luna, and only the paid re-send measures it.\n    42\t\n    43\t## 3. Not done\n    44\t\n    45\tNothing was paid. Nothing was tuned on the 36 stored answers. The request was not changed after the registration.\n    27\t  - F5, the outside total-capex range, is marked as the market's reference for data-center spending. The Codex adjudicator says this\n    28\t    implies a 100% share. The Claude adjudicator says F5 is a quoted outside figure, as the reference rule allows, and that the\n    29\t    deduction is set out separately.\n    30\t- **The reasons and premises** behind every settled S and E vote were read and recorded (`reasons.json`). X05 and X08's readers read\n    31\t  the same four companies' AI-infrastructure plan as a plan that can slip, not as a floor, which fits each answer's own outcome.\n    32\t- **A run incident, disclosed.** The background command running the eight Codex readers was terminated (exit 144) while three\n    33\t  readers were still running. X04, X05 and X08 had empty outputs, kept in `reader-packets/interrupted/`. Fresh Codex readers were\n    34\t  run for those three. No reading was discarded after it was seen.\n    35\t\n    36\t## 2. The E record across the four runs (same case, same passages)\n    37\t\n    38\t| Run | Codex writer | Claude writer |\n    39\t|---|---|---|\n    40\t| third run (D742 request) | 85% realisation, $34B; settled no | 1.85x bracket, $37B; yes in re-read 2, split under D747 |\n    41\t| targeted test (D745) | 90% realisation, $36B; settled no | 1.8x × 90% share, $32.4B; split, then no under D747 |\n    42\t| D749 | unresolved, specific gap; settled yes | 2.1x in full, $42B, asserting \"already selling at volume\"; split |\n    43\t| D750 | unresolved, specific gap; settled yes | 1.75x bracket midpoint, $35B; settled no |\n    44\t\n    45\t- **The Codex writer.** The repair (D749, D750) moved it from invented cuts to a justified unresolved, twice.\n    46\t- **The Claude writer.** It forecasts every time, by a different route each time, and has never been settled supported under the\n    47\t  current definitions.\n    48\t- **S** improved but did not fully settle (corrected after council 67):\n    49\t  - D750's two fresh forecasts earned settled support, with two defect marks on X08 still split.\n    50\t  - D749's X08 support verdict was split after the correction: one reader accepted $710.4B while treating $730B as a floor.\n    51\t  - D749's S Codex was settled supported.\n    52\t- **P-v8** has been supported throughout.\n    53\t\n    54\t## 3. Not done\n    55\t\n    56\tNothing was paid. Nothing was tuned on the 36 stored answers. The request was not changed after the registration.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md | sed -n '12,48p;236,285p' && rg -n 'P-v8|clean|settled failure' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md | tail -30\"","aggregated_output":"    12\t| pair-old-2 (v14, v9) | joint; separate-v14; separate-v9 | as above | 237 |\n    13\t| pair-old-3 (v3, v35) | joint; separate-v3; separate-v35 | as above | 260 |\n    14\t| single-O (old-2 v29, equity commitments) | single | v29 | its own request's (35) |\n    15\t| single-S (old-2 v6, four hyperscalers' 2027 capex) | single | v6 | 31 |\n    16\t| single-E (old-3 v41, FY2028 Vera CPU) | single | v41 | 15 |\n    17\t\n    18\t- **Each request:** judging-v13's system text (`judging-v13-draft.txt`, 4,373 characters); the family header with the exact outcome\n    19\t  keys; the evidence; the themes once. No earlier forecast is shown (D728). The strict v13 schema.\n    20\t- **The model:** poc-v118's re-judging setting (gpt-6-luna, medium effort, 16,000 output tokens).\n    21\t- **Draws:** three per request, so 36 first attempts.\n    22\t- **Retry:** a calculation the checks refuse, or a schema-invalid answer, is retried once with the reasons. A second refusal makes the\n    23\t  unit unresolved.\n    24\t- **What is scored:** the first attempt, on both arms (§3f). Retries are sent and recorded for diagnosis only.\n    25\t- **The separate arm:** its two first answers per pair and draw are merged and checked over the pair's family (C3, C4 across the two).\n    26\t\n    27\t## 2. Spend and stops\n    28\t\n    29\t- **The ledger:** one, at Alex's ceiling (`paid_gate.py send <folder> <ceiling> paid-plan.json` refuses any other ceiling).\n    30\t- **Budget:** the real budget transport and stop gate, which reserve each request's worst case before sending. After a stop, nothing\n    31\t  more is sent, and the remaining units are recorded as not sent.\n    32\t- **Estimate** (at `hivemind.model.PRICES`: gpt-6-luna, $0.10 input and $0.50 output per million tokens; tokens estimated as\n    33\t  characters / 4):\n    34\t  - a pair request is about 34–38k input tokens and a singleton 6–9k;\n    35\t  - the 36 first attempts cost about $0.17 at 4,000 output tokens each, and at most $0.39 if every answer used its 16,000-token cap;\n    36\t  - the transport reserves at most $0.039 for one request.\n    37\t  - **Proposed ceiling: $0.50.** It covers every scored attempt. Retries come last (§3g), so a ceiling stop among them leaves the\n    38\t    scored test complete.\n    39\t- **Sends:** one at a time, every first attempt before any retry (§3g). Each send has its own archive, so no draw is answered from\n    40\t  another.\n    41\t\n    42\t## 3. The $0 rehearsal (`paid_rehearsal.py`, `rehearsal.log`: 16 of 16)\n    43\t\n    44\t- **Clean run:** 36 first attempts, every HTTP body byte-equal to an independently built request, every unit published, the separate\n    45\t  arm reconciled in 9 of 9, the spend settled under the ceiling.\n    46\t- **Paths:**\n    47\t  - a refused calculation is retried, then published;\n    48\t  - two refusals give unresolved;\n   236\t## 3h. After council round 47g (`codex-r47g.md`)\n   237\t\n   238\t- **[47g] Two journals, read on the writer's own delimiter.**\n   239\t  - Scored first attempts are written to `answers.jsonl`, and diagnostic retries to `retries.jsonl`. The run digest covers both.\n   240\t  - A damaged line in `answers.jsonl` makes the verdict INCOMPLETE. A damaged line in `retries.jsonl` is reported and marks that retry\n   241\t    interrupted, but leaves the scored test complete.\n   242\t  - Records are split on '\\n' only, the writer's delimiter. An answer may contain other Unicode line separators (U+2028, U+0085,\n   243\t    U+2029), and these are kept inside the record.\n   244\t  - Rehearsed: a torn retry record leaves the verdict complete; a torn scored record (the existing control) makes it INCOMPLETE; an\n   245\t    answer holding U+2028, U+0085 and U+2029 stays one record; the order of every first attempt before any retry is checked on the\n   246\t    provider's own call sequence.\n   247\t- **Rehearsal:** 63 of 63, three runs (`rehearsal.log`).\n   248\t- **Mutants:** 33 of 33 caught, and the control passes (`paid-mutants.log`).\n   249\t  - One mutant (splitting on any line break) is caught when a scenario guard stops the rehearsal: 36 attempts started, 35 finished.\n   250\t  - The mutant harness now counts such a stop as a catch and shows its reason.\n   251\t\n   252\t## 4. The reading rule (fixed now, before any answer exists)\n   253\t\n   254\t- **Packets:** 27 (`paid_gate.py packets`): 9 joint pair-draws, 9 reconciled separate pair-draws and 9 singleton draws. The labels are\n   255\t  shuffled, the arm is hidden, and the key is kept in `~/.hivemind-keys/v13-paid-gate/`.\n   256\t  - Each packet shows, per card (K1, K2), the question, the ladder, every passage the final answer names (as the request showed it),\n   257\t    its figures, the recomputed outcome, the recorded assumptions and the card. Nothing about attempts or the code's decision is\n   258\t    shown (§3e).\n   259\t  - A packet the code did not publish (held, unresolved, not reconciled) is read too. The verdict, not the reader, applies the code's\n   260\t    decision: its cards cannot count as usable.\n   261\t  - The readers receive `packets.md` exactly, with its key-commitment line. The readings file carries that text's sha256.\n   262\t- **Readers:** two fresh readers of different families, a Codex session and a Claude subagent, each blind to the other and to the arm.\n   263\t  For each card each answers four questions, yes or no, with the passage that decides:\n   264\t  1. **usable:** the outcome is usable and supported by the shown passages it rests on;\n   265\t  2. **contradiction:** with the other card of its packet, it states a hard numerical contradiction;\n   266\t  3. **internal_reference:** the system's own forecast is presented as the market's expectation;\n   267\t  4. **deciding_gap:** an unresolved overlap, a change of scope or an unsized adjustment decides its band.\n   268\t- **Agreement:** where the two readers agree, that is the reading. Where they disagree, a third fresh reader (Codex) reads the card with\n   269\t  both readings and the passages, and its answer decides. No reading is changed after the verdict is computed.\n   270\t- **Verdict** (`paid_gate.py verdict`, D725's lines, on the first attempts; computed once and sealed, §3f):\n   271\t  - **PASS:** the joint arm has both cards usable in at least 8 of 9 pairs, at least 2 of 3 per snapshot, and at least 3 more usable\n   272\t    pairs than the separate arm; and zero published defects (contradiction, internal reference or deciding gap) across the joint and\n   273\t    singleton answers.\n   274\t  - **STOP:** any such published defect; a coverage line missed; a gain under 3.\n   275\t  - **INCOMPLETE:** a packet or a card missing a reading, or a unit not sent.\n   276\t  - This is a small design test, not release acceptance.\n   277\t\n   278\t## 5. What the result decides\n   279\t\n   280\t- **PASS:** judging-v13 earns a production design: automatic families in matching, lifecycle and the request builder, each a further\n   281\t  step Alex opens.\n   282\t- **STOP or INCOMPLETE:** the line goes back to Alex with the readings, and nothing is tuned on these 36 answers.\n168:| P (joint), P-v4, P-v8 (separate) | old-1 v4 8810, v8 8816 | NVIDIA revenue company FY2027; NVIDIA revenue Data Center FY2027 | 431.2/392/352.8/313.6; 400/350/300/250 | the pair; 79 quotes absent from the gate |\n276:**A clean result** goes to Alex as the request for the paid re-send: the same 36 requests rebuilt in v13s, under a new decision with\n323:  - A clean re-read goes to Alex for the paid re-send, beside the third run's record and its disclosures.\n348:  - A clean result goes to Alex for the paid re-send.\n364:  - cases S, E and P-v8;\n373:  | K-unresolved-unjustified | P-v8 Claude made unresolved with a vague reason | not supported |\n377:  - both P-v8 replies have a settled \"yes\" on a forecast;\n383:  - a clean result supports asking Alex to authorize the 36-request paid re-send.\n405:  | blind4 U01: P-v8, vague unresolved control | not supported |\n439:  - A clean re-read does not turn D745 into a pass.\n464:  - cases S, E and P-v8, built from the repaired request;\n482:  - both fresh P-v8 replies have a settled yes on a forecast;\n489:    - a fresh P-v8 reply that is not a forecast (decided from its shape alone);\n498:  - Only a clean pass supports asking Alex to authorize the paid re-send.\n500:  - a clean set passes, with the floor marked by readers who listed its figures differently;\n505:  - a fresh P-v8 answered unresolved fails;\n526:  - S, E and P-v8, each with one fresh Claude and one fresh read-only Codex writer;\n534:  - a clean pass supports asking Alex to authorize the paid re-send;\n576:  - S, E and P-v8, each with one fresh Claude and one fresh read-only Codex writer;\n581:  - a clean pass goes to Alex for the paid authorization;\n582:  - a settled failure or a persistent split ends this wording loop, and the publication choice for E-type cases returns to Alex.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE-RESULT.md | head -100 && rg -n 'luna|MODEL|SNAPSHOTS|SINGLETONS' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE-RESULT.md | head -100 && rg -n 'luna|MODEL|SNAPSHOTS|SINGLETONS' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# judging-v13 paid gate: result (D733)\n     2\t\n     3\t**STOP.** Every one of the 72 answers was refused by the code checks: 36 scored first attempts and 36 diagnostic retries. Nothing\n     4\twas published, and the joint arm is usable in 0 of 9 pairs against the registered 8 of 9.\n     5\t\n     6\tThe result does not answer the question the gate was built for: does judging related number questions in one call give better\n     7\tnumbers than judging each alone? It shows that the answer format, as the request stated it, could not be filled. Most refusals are\n     8\trules the request never stated, or checks that refuse right answers. A few are the model's own errors.\n     9\t\n    10\t- **Run:** `poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22/`, sent once (D732) with gpt-6-luna. The ledger records 72 attempts and\n    11\t  $0.3677 of the $0.50 ceiling: $0.1554 for the first attempts and $0.2124 for the retries. Every attempt finished with status\n    12\t  `answered`, so the result is complete, not INCOMPLETE.\n    13\t- **The blind reading** was not run. The registered brief says \"A card that has no answer cannot be usable\", and every card has\n    14\t  none, so the readers cannot change the verdict.\n    15\t- **Council round 48:**\n    16\t  - `round48-brief.md`;\n    17\t  - `claude-r48.md`, Claude's reflection, written first;\n    18\t  - `codex-r48.md`, an independent fresh read-only Codex session that reproduced every refusal from the journals and the requests.\n    19\t\n    20\t## 1. Census (first attempt → retry; Codex's count, which corrects Claude's)\n    21\t\n    22\t| Attempt | Arm | Answers | Structure (C7) messages / answers | Stopped at the structure check |\n    23\t|---|---|---:|---:|---:|\n    24\t| First | Joint | 9 | 39 / 9 | 9 |\n    25\t| First | Separate | 18 | 47 / 18 | 17 |\n    26\t| First | Single | 9 | 13 / 9 | 8 |\n    27\t| Retry | Joint | 9 | 40 / 9 | 9 |\n    28\t| Retry | Separate | 18 | 41 / 18 | 14 |\n    29\t| Retry | Single | 9 | 12 / 9 | 6 |\n    30\t\n    31\t`check()` returns early after any structure error, so most source, arithmetic and compatibility checks never ran on these answers.\n    32\t\n    33\t**Prose where the check expects an id**, counted by occurrence:\n    34\t\n    35\t| Field | First | Retry |\n    36\t|---|---:|---:|\n    37\t| `answers[].outcome` | 45 of 45 | 45 of 45 |\n    38\t| `answers[].reference.figure` | 39 | 32 |\n    39\t| `figures[].evidence` (139 of them on source figures, where C1 needs a passage id) | 175 | 133 (95) |\n    40\t| `figures[].bridge` | 49 | 44 |\n    41\t| `converts`, `disputed`, `inputs` | 3 | 3 |\n    42\t\n    43\t**Not one of the 90 member answers named a figure as its outcome**, in either journal. This failure alone refuses every answer.\n    44\tThe retries did not repair it. They carried the first attempt's reasons, and the early return had hidden this reason on most\n    45\tanswers.\n    46\t\n    47\t## 2. Mechanism\n    48\t\n    49\t1. **The format's rules live in `calc_check.py`, not in the request.**\n    50\t   - The sent schema carries 7 descriptions: `what`, `basis`, `kind`, `expression`, `relation`, `bridge` and `reference`.\n    51\t   - These fields have none: `value`, `low`, `high`, `bound`, `unit`, `evidence`, `component`, `inputs`, `operation`, `signs`,\n    52\t     `disputed`, `basis_text`, `rests_on`, `converts`, `scenario`, `outcome` and `rung`.\n    53\t   - The check enforces closed conventions on them:\n    54\t     - ids, not prose, in `evidence`, `outcome`, `reference.figure`, `bridge` and `disputed`;\n    55\t     - the unit spellings `USD bn`, `%` and `x`;\n    56\t     - a value or a range, never both (all 52 such refusals gave both);\n    57\t     - the period label `H1`;\n    58\t     - `share`, not `product`, for a percentage;\n    59\t     - each operation's operand count;\n    60\t     - `signs` only on `sum` and `roll_forward`.\n    61\t   - The descriptions of `bridge` (\"an estimate stating the conversion …\") and `reference` (\"An external figure, or a calculation …\")\n    62\t     read as asking for prose. The check reads both as ids.\n    63\t   - REQUEST-AUDIT.md says \"field definitions moved to the schema\". Most did not reach the sent bytes.\n    64\t2. **Some checks refuse right answers.** Each case was exposed by replaying a stored answer with the earlier refusal removed on a\n    65\t   copy:\n    66\t   - Q1 + Q2 Data Center revenue = \"FY2027 first half\" is refused, because the period parser reads only `H1`.\n    67\t   - FY2026 Q4 × (1 + the source's stated 65% year-over-year growth) is refused for want of a bridge. The check excuses\n    68\t     `apply_growth` only under a family `same_base` relation.\n    69\t   - $411 billion × 92.5% is computed by `product` as 38,017.5.\n    70\t   - \"well over $300 billion\" is refused because the parser starts one word later.\n    71\t   - $1.05 trillion is compared with bands in billions.\n    72\t3. **Model errors seen in the sample:**\n    73\t   - a 15% growth rate supported by forecasts computed from that same rate, a cycle;\n    74\t   - $25 billion and $18 billion of commitments added, although the source does not establish that the $18 billion comes on top of\n    75\t     the $25 billion;\n    76\t   - a full-year residual taken as a forecast. This is a judgement for a reader, not for code.\n    77\t4. **Diagnostic on copies (Claude's script, labelled a probe, not a count of right answers).**\n    78\t   - The script mechanically corrected the format classes.\n    79\t   - Result: 25 of 36 first attempts passed the early structure check, 9 had no structure error at all, and 0 would publish. The\n    80\t     retries gave the same counts.\n    81\t   - It guesses links from matching amounts, so it does not show how many answers a repaired request would get right.\n    82\t\n    83\t## 3. Process error\n    84\t\n    85\tAGENTS §6 says: \"Check before the send, at no cost. Write one answer that follows the prompt and run every code check on it.\"\n    86\t\n    87\t- **What the $0 gate proved.** Its passing answer, like the rehearsal's clean fixture, was written by someone who knew the check's\n    88\t  ids, units and period spellings. It proved that the harness accepts a conforming answer, not that the request teaches one.\n    89\t- **The missed step.** A writer who sees only the outgoing request writes the answer, and that first output goes through the real\n    90\t  checks. Each refusal must then point to the sentence of the request that states its rule.\n    91\t- **What the eight council rounds covered.** Rounds 47 to 47h reviewed the harness. None read the request as the model reads it\n    92\t  against the check.\n    93\t- **A wrong field in the mid-run diagnosis.** Claude told Alex the prose sat in `rests_on`. It sat in `evidence`, `bridge`, `outcome`\n    94\t  and `reference`.\n    95\t\n    96\t## 4. Council consensus: one recommendation, one test (for Alex's choice)\n    97\t\n    98\t**Recommendation.** Keep the STOP. Alex opens one bounded repair of judging-v13's request and checks:\n    99\t- each field's rule goes in the schema beside the field;\n   100\t- ids, units and choices become enums where the strict schema can hold them (the request already uses an id enum for `cites`);\n33:CONFIG = REPO / 'poc/config/poc-v118.json'                              # its re-judging model setting: gpt-6-luna, medium, 16000 output tokens\n43:SINGLETONS = [('O', 'old-2', 'v29', 8734, ('NVIDIA', 'equity investment commitments', 'company', 'end of FY2027')),\n150:    for name, draw, view_id, call_id, what in SINGLETONS:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '96,201p' docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md && tail -3 docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '96,201p' docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md && tail -3 docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md\"","aggregated_output":"\n1. **Replace the abstract sentence \"makes the answer more or less likely through demand, supply, competition or pricing\" with the few classes in which an analyst recognises an indirect relation, written as a closed set, each class with two or three examples from other industries** (AGENTS §6: one\n   abstract sentence is a coin flip on every send; examples replace it). A first draft of the classes (checked against the standard line by line at\n   design time; it holds only meanings the standard already has):\n   - The question's own figure: this question's figures, guidance, estimates and revisions (estimates for another period or another population follow\n     the existing rule).\n   - The other side of the same money: a seller's revenue from the buyers the question counts; those buyers' orders and backlog with these sellers.\n   - Price and volume: a product's price or shipments, for the question on that product's revenue.\n   - Variants and parts: a product's variants, alternatives, key parts and schedule changes, for the question on that product's launch.\n   - Demand, supply and competition moves with a number: customers' orders and capital-spending plans, suppliers' capacity and prices, competitors'\n     sales and share, for the question they affect.\n2. **State how to file a fact when two specific questions both bear on it** (the problem the `definition_no_fallback` arm exposed: Morgan Stanley's rack\n   price was filed under the buyers' capital-spending question, and the seller's Rubin revenue question lost it). One home per fact (D207): file it under\n   the question its figure measures most directly (for example, a hotel's room rate goes under its room-revenue question, not under the\n   travellers'-spending question; a parts maker's revenue from carmakers goes under the carmakers' parts-spending question, not under a rival parts\n   maker's share question); when one sentence gives a separate figure for each of two questions, write two items, each filed under its own question\n   (code stores two items that quote the same sentence as one record linked to both questions); never also file it under a broader question about the\n   same company (the failure of D355's test 2: the model filed it under an NVIDIA revenue question as well). The original draft's \"when the other\n   party's question is open … file it there as well\" conflicted with D207 and was deleted on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement (D360).\n3. **Delete the fallback sentence** and return to the standard's §6: when no question takes a fact, it is not filed; code drops it as \"no home\" and\n   records it (D209), and evaluation reads each one.\n4. Change only the one place that holds the filing rule; report its length in characters before and after, and write down the reason for any growth\n   (this time's reason: examples replace one abstract sentence).\n\n## 6. How to measure (the ruler does not move; the method does not change again)\n\n- **Requests**: rebuild each stored request byte for byte with the production code, changing only the one place that holds the filing rule; the\n  rebuilt request's archive id must equal the original's, or it is not sent (`resend_filing.py`, AGENTS §5). Never re-send the archive's copy whose\n  keys were sorted alphabetically.\n- **Articles**: development set = the 54 articles of `selection.json` (already read many times); held-out set = articles the extraction gold read and\n  the judgement gold uses that are not among those 54, drawn once before the test, with the list frozen and never changed afterwards. Both sets are\n  measured and reported apart.\n- **Control**: the unchanged requests re-sent 3 times the same day. No comparison with the archive (model drift).\n- **Each answer**: goes through the production code checks and filing first (including \"keep only the narrowest\" and \"no home means dropped\"), and is\n  counted after.\n- **What is counted** (`measure_filing.py`, with its matching rule fixed: items are matched on the distinct numbers and the proper nouns other than\n  NVIDIA in the source text; a source passage the system quotes that overlaps the gold's passage also counts as the same matter; \"optional\" counts as\n  optional; the citations in the body of the gold's answers are read):\n  - whether each fact that matters (one the gold uses to judge any question) reached the question that uses it, counted per fact, not per \"fact ×\n    question\";\n  - per question: the facts that matter that reached it and the new noise that appeared, counted by the decision rule below;\n  - the parking lot: how many facts the total-revenue question (v1, together with v22) takes in, and what they are;\n  - noise: how many of the sentences the standard does not keep are kept; per question, how noise is filed (links on facts the standard does not keep\n    or no question uses);\n  - how many core and supporting facts appear; the facts that matter that are dropped; how many questions each fact is filed under on average.\n  - Matching still has a small error (spot check: the new rule mismatches a few and misses a few); say so when reporting the numbers, and read the\n    ones that matter one by one.\n- **Decision rule** (rewritten on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement, D360. The original draft required \"every draw above the highest of the control's\n  three\" and the like, which the control itself fails when measured again, so it cannot tell good from bad. Script `decide.py`; written down and frozen\n  before each test; each draw's numbers and the totals are all reported):\n  - **Totals, with luck ruled out first**: each article's 6 answers from the new and the old arm are randomly reassigned between the two arms 4,000\n    times (with a fixed random seed); the new rule's difference counts as a real change only when fewer than 5% of the reassignments reach it.\n    - Development set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must rise; the count under the total-revenue questions (v1, v22) must\n      fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core facts must not fall.\n    - Held-out set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must not fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core\n      facts must not fall.\n  - **Per question, counted by code against the gold** (development and held-out sets together):\n    - A fact that matters which reaches the question at least two fewer times under the new rule than under the control counts as \"one fewer\" for that\n      question; in under the control all three times and under the new rule none of the three, it counts as \"lost outright\". A move to another question\n      that the gold also uses it for and that is no broader than the original (one home per fact, D207) does not count as fewer; a move to a broader\n      question counts. \"One more\" in the opposite direction offsets it.\n    - A noise sentence (not kept by the standard, or kept but used by no question) that the new rule files under the question at least twice while the\n      control never does counts as \"new noise\"; filed all three times, it counts as \"outright new noise\". The opposite direction offsets it.\n    - Any question with a net 3 fewer, a net 2 lost outright, a net 5 more new noise, or a net 3 more outright new noise fails. These thresholds are the\n      smallest values that keep \"misjudged when new and old are exactly the same\" within about one in ten; at the test, first compute the\n      misjudgement rate from the reassignments; if it is above one in ten, raise the threshold most prone to misjudgement by one step, then compare with\n      what was measured.\n  - **Smaller changes** (a question 2 fewer, two or three more noise) cannot be told from luck in three measurements: all are listed, given to a reader\n    who did not write the rule to read against the raw answers, and written into the report; they do not decide pass or fail.\n  Only when everything passes does it become a candidate; a candidate runs end to end three times on the 14-day window (extraction re-sent, matching and\n  judging run as usual, scored on the frozen judgement ruler), and that step needs Alex's word.\n\n## 7. Process (no drift)\n\n- **One recommendation and one test at a time** (AGENTS §7.3, Alex: \"就测试一种，一次\"): write the design first and put it through rounds of review (own\n  checks, Codex read-only, a reader who did not write it reading the raw records) until a round finds nothing serious; write down and freeze the\n  predictions and stop conditions; test once; if it does not match, stop and take it to Alex (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\").\n- **A review's report is a lead**: each finding counts only once it is checked against the records (AGENTS §1).\n- **A prompt audit before any prompt change** (`.claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md`): check the whole request against the standard sentence by\n  sentence, in both directions.\n- **Every step goes into `log.md`**: what was done, what came of it, the files; mistakes and overturned results stay in it too.\n- **Do not repeat what was ruled out** (§3).\n\n## 8. Steps and where we are\n\n| # | Step | Output | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Design: the classes of indirect relation (a closed set + examples from other industries), how to file when a fact measures both sides, deleting the fallback; checked against the standard sentence by sentence; length before and after | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/design-links.md` | Done (draft 3, v74l / poc-v115) |\n| 2 | Checks at no cost: a sample answer written by the new rule goes through every code check; the new rule's text goes through the prompt audit | Same as above | Done |\n| 3 | Review rounds (Codex, independent readers, own), until nothing serious remains | `review-links-*.md` | Done (Codex 7 rounds, independent readers 3 times; round 7 found nothing serious) |\n| 4 | Draw and freeze the held-out set; write down and freeze this test's predictions and decision rule | `heldout.json`, `predictions-links.md` | Done (sha256 recorded for both) |\n| 5 | Test once: development set + held-out set, the new rule and the same-day control 3 times each | `answers-links.json`, `log.md` | Done (2026-11-04): failed: facts that matter reaching the right question, development set +7 (p 0.26); every other condition passed |\n| 6 | If passed: to Alex, to decide whether to run three 14-day end-to-end draws; if failed: stop, to Alex | `design-links.md` §8 | Stopped, to Alex |\n| 7 | (D361, Alex: \"按照你的提以来，但是你一定是自己反思多轮之后才给出的行动\") Measure the ceiling first: hand the source passages the gold cites straight to the frozen judgement; the custom-chip, operating-margin and capital-spending questions judged 3 times each at the window end, the custom-chip question as it was 3 times as the control; the fallback sentence is not deleted for now | `reflection-v74l.md`, `oracle-design.md` | Done: operating margin grounded 3/3, capital spending 2/3, custom chips 0/3 (the gap is in interpretation); the next step waits for Alex |\n| 8 | (D362, Alex: \"你继续啊不要scope drift and strongly follow evidence of your discovery and learning\") Two small changes where facts are not delivered, one at a time: ① when a quote is rewritten, code matches it back to the source sentence (changing nothing the model sees; verified by replaying the archive); ② a figure question's description states which figures it is derived from by definition (first sent 3 times each on the article that motivated it) | `delivery-fixes.md` | Stopped: an independent check overturned the first change's code (it would take in wrong figures); the second failed its trial send (1/3). The root: the signed standard's §5.3 does not keep two key facts, treating them as \"a restatement of known facts\" (proposal 6, awaiting approval); to Alex |\n| 9 | **Historical; superseded by D370.** (D363, Alex: \"乙\") At coverage start, record each question's starting figure once from the company's latest earnings report; the daily path still takes no restatements: design, rounds of self-checks and independent checks, validated first on the archive in the simplest way | `baseline-design.md` | Validated on two questions: the frozen extraction does not read the report's tables (0/3); with each question reading the report once on its own, the operating-margin question read its starting figure 2/3 and the capital-spending question 3/3, and the judgement then judged right (2/3, 3/3), about 2.5 cents. All 22 questions tested once (2026-11-05, Alex: \"你充分验证\"; files chosen by code, a same-day control, about $0.43): v10 and v12 judged right 9/9 each; no question already judged right turned wrong; but 5 of the 53 figures read were other quantities (the Vera CPU question and the Blackwell and Rubin question took a larger or neighbouring revenue line), so it failed condition 3, written beforehand. The model itself pointed to the reading instructions' sentence \"write the lines it is computed from, or the line the report uses for it\"; stopped, to Alex. Alex: \"改\"; only this sentence was changed (\"write only the lines it is computed from, or the same quantity under another name; never a broader line or a line next to it\"), tried on 4 questions first, then tested on all 22 (about $0.36): all three conditions passed: v10 and v12 9/9 each, no question judged right turned wrong, and all 46 figures were the questions' own quantities. Checked after an independent reader's challenge: the new sentence made the reading miss the capital-spending line in 3 of 12 reads, and it had been tested only on the one May report; the fourth version (a line-by-line re-check after writing) removed too much and failed; after a census of every failure trace (Alex: \"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信\"), the third version with the repaired quote check was chosen (D365): 0 wrong figures on the held-out August report; built into the production system (poc-v116; the $0 checks and the tests all pass). The full acceptance, three 14-day draws (Alex: \"跑验收\"; a same-day control; about $4.79): all passed: operating margin and capital spending judged right 3/3 each (the control never judged them), 53 against 45 in total (of 57); for the China-share question, the only one two fewer, both readers found the starting figures did not lead it astray. Alex: \"都做\": poc-v116 became the default (D366); the code was committed and a PR opened |\n\n## 9. Alex's own words on this matter (in time order)\n\n- \"你这个单独开一个专题出来解决 … 是promot，是系统结构设计还是到底哪里有问题挂错一堆东西，机制是不是有问题（一定要挂吗？有没有可能是杂音\" (2026-11-03)\n- \"So if it's a customer problem or other companies that you think is relating NVIDIA and it's the wrong evidence, then what is the standard for it? … from a hedge fund analyst standpoint, are these even relevant? Do you think your ruler is wrong in this aspect?\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"你确定下你接下来要改进的量化目标，以及你要看到端到端我们的管线是要做什么的，不要乱硬优化，要看到目标\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"就测试一种，一次 … 能不能自己有个超级自信的定论和证据再给我提建议\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"这些只是类型题，你需要抽象拔高我们现在的核心问题和卡点。这个很重要这样改下去才是对的\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"go\" (2026-11-04, on the proposed amendments to §5.2 and §6 and on this test)\n| 39 | 11-05 | 第 3 步：做进正式系统（设计四张表，Codex 审 3 轮：第 1 轮 4 条严重、第 2 轮 2 条严重，都改；第 3 轮无严重问题） | 建成 `baseline.run` + poc-v116 + baseline-v3 提示词文件；$0 核对：44 份请求和验证时逐字节相同，3 次存下的回答走正式代码写出的记录完全一样，第一天判断正好判这些题，网页从开始跟踪那天可见；新测试 12 条、6 个弄坏版本都抓出、全套 272 条通过；设计页和 PRD 00/03/04/05/06 更新，`docs/check.py` 通过 | 交 Alex：要不要跑 14 天三遍完整验收（约 5 美元） |\n| 40 | 11-05 | Alex：\"跑验收\"。14 天三遍完整验收：poc-v116（开始跟踪时读财报）三遍、当天重跑 poc-v108 三遍作对照，冻结尺子打分（事先锁定 `acceptance-design.md`）；电脑中途在电池上睡了约 5 小时，6 遍被暂停，插电后自己接着跑完 | **全过**：v10、v12 各 3/3（对照从没判过）；合计 53 对 45（满分 57，+8）；唯一少两次的 v17（0/3 对 2/3）两人都认定不是起点数：同一份抽取请求，新配置两遍碰巧留下\"中国份额归零\"那句，对照没留；约 4.79 美元 | 交 Alex：poc-v116 是否成为默认 |\n| 41 | 11-05 | Alex：\"都做\"（poc-v116 设成默认；提交代码、开 PR）。默认配置改成 poc-v116，默认的条目数据库换成带财报网页原文的那一份（旧的没有原文，新默认会在它上面拒跑）；测试按用户的用法补上：不给种子题就拒绝，给了就先读财报再跑第一天 | 测试全过，5 个弄坏的版本都抓出；配置的设置和验收三遍记下的校验码一致（只改了配置里的注释）；花钱前的检查脚本在新配置的运行数据上照常跑完，但不复查读财报那一步的引用检查（记为未补的缺口）；D366 | 提交、开 PR；下一类问题等 Alex |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
